Reject continue outside a loop, as break already is. Only break
checked that it was inside a while or for, so a continue outside any
loop took the top of an empty stack of loop labels and compiled a jump
to whatever word lay before the stack's buffer. At top level, running
the jump crashed bc; in the body of a function that was never called,
the garbage was silently accepted. Check for an enclosing loop for
both keywords, so that continue there is a parse error ("bad token").

---
 miscutils/bc.c |    9 ++++-----
 1 file changed, 4 insertions(+), 5 deletions(-)

--- a/miscutils/bc.c
+++ b/miscutils/bc.c
@@ -4423,13 +4423,12 @@
 	BcParse *p = &G.prs;
 	size_t i;
 
-	if (type == BC_LEX_KEY_BREAK) {
-		if (p->exits.len == 0) // none of the enclosing blocks is a loop
-			RETURN_STATUS(bc_error_bad_token());
+	if (p->exits.len == 0) // none of the enclosing blocks is a loop
+		RETURN_STATUS(bc_error_bad_token());
+	if (type == BC_LEX_KEY_BREAK)
 		i = *(size_t*)bc_vec_top(&p->exits);
-	} else {
+	else
 		i = *(size_t*)bc_vec_top(&p->conds);
-	}
 	bc_parse_pushJUMP(i);
 
 	RETURN_STATUS(zxc_lex_next());
